+# The SDK: what a caller sees
+
+The cluster's rules — the leader, the read index, the idempotency key, the retry window — are
+somebody else's problem. This page is the last step of that sentence: the API a caller uses, and the
+three decisions that make it an SDK rather than a thin wrapper over the wire.
+
+## The error type is the contract
+
+A caller has to be able to tell these apart, because the remedies are different:
+
+| error | what the caller does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `NotLeader { leader }` | retry — the SDK will, at the node it names |
+| `Unavailable { detail }` | retry later: the cluster may come back |
+| `Refused { reason }` | do not retry: the store has decided |
+| `RetriesExhausted { attempts }` | give up, and report the last refusal |
+
+An `Error::Io` with a message is a contract that says nothing; `is_retryable()` is the two-line
+version of the table — `NotLeader` and `Unavailable`, nothing else. `Refused` and `RetriesExhausted`
+are the SDK's own verdicts, and telling a caller to retry an answer the SDK just gave up on is the
+one thing the method must not do.
+
+## The retries are the SDK's
+
+Following a redirect (`5.7`), retrying with the same idempotency key (`5.8`), and stopping after a
+bound are *one* policy. In the SDK it is a policy every caller gets; in the caller it is a policy
+every caller invents, and one of them forgets the key.
+
+The bound is `MAX_ATTEMPTS`, and it is a number rather than a hope: a cluster that never names a
+leader must produce `RetriesExhausted`, not a client that spins.
+
+## A write that was applied is a success
+
+The SDK does not tell "applied now" from "applied before" at the API level. The caller asked for a
+write; the write exists; whether it took one attempt or three is an implementation detail of the
+retry policy. `5.8`'s `Outcome` keeps the distinction for the tests that want it, and the SDK folds
+both into "ok".
+
+## Two implementations, one suite
+
+`conformance@2` runs the same suite against two implementations of the backend port: a **scripted**
+one (a table and a ledger — the smallest thing that satisfies the port) and an **in-process** one
+(the real `Raft`, the real state machine, the real ledger). A policy that passes against one and
+fails against the other is a policy that depends on an accident of the implementation, which is
+exactly what the gate is for.
+
+Running the suite by hand:
+
+```sh
+KILN_BACKEND=scripted   cargo test -p meshkv --test spec_client_sdk
+KILN_BACKEND=in-process cargo test -p meshkv --test spec_client_sdk
+```
